Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program that utilizes some advanced features like Promises, async/await, destructuring, arrow functions, and the Fetch API.

```javascript
// Simulate fetching data with a promise-based function
const fetchData = async () => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        setTimeout(() => {
            resolve({
                user: { id: 1, name: 'John Doe' },
                posts: [{ id: 101, title: 'Async/Await in JS' }, { id: 102, title: 'Understanding Promises' }]
            });
        }, 1000);
    });
};

// Using async/await to handle the promise and destructuring to extract the needed properties
const displayData = async () => {
    try {
        const { user, posts } = await fetchData();
        const { id, name } = user;

        console.log(`User: ${name} (ID: ${id})`);

        posts.forEach(({ id: postId, title }) => {
            console.log(`Post ${postId}: ${title}`);
        });

    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
    }
};

displayData();
```

This code defines a function `fetchData` that simulates an API call using a Promise. It then uses the `displayData` function to fetch this data asynchronously with `async/await`, destructure the returned objects, and logs the results to the console.